<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mantingan Mosque</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1559">1559</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mantingan Mosque is a mosque near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jepara">Jepara</a> on the north coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-java">Central Java</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, built in 1559 under the patronage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ratu-kalinyamat">Ratu Kalinyamat</a>. It is known for its carved stone panels with floral and landscape designs, a blend of Hindu-Javanese and Islamic art. The tomb of her husband, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan-hadlirin">Sultan Hadlirin</a>, lies in its grounds.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mantingan-mosque.jpg" alt="Mantingan Mosque" width="320" height="222" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AnsyahF · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Masjid_Mantingan_Jepara.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
